stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Requested: CRM raises Unit Change Request\n(old_transaction_id, old_unit_id, new_unit_id, reason)

    Requested --> Mgmt_Pending: submitted
    Mgmt_Pending --> Approved: Management Approves
    Mgmt_Pending --> Requested: Management Sends Back (remark mandatory)

    Approved --> [*]: New Transaction created (old transaction historically preserved)

    note right of Approved
        The OLD transaction is historically preserved throughout: its
        original unit_id, booking/financial baseline, documents, and
        workflow/audit history are never overwritten or reassigned.
        Only its lifecycle status changes. On approval:
        1. Old Transaction.lifecycle_status: ACTIVE → SUPERSEDED
           (unit_id itself is never changed — it stays the
           permanent historical record for its original unit)
        2. New Transaction created fresh:
           - unit_id = new_unit_id
           - source_transaction_id = old_transaction.id
           - source_change_type = 'UNIT_CHANGE'
           - lifecycle_status = ACTIVE
           - booking/financial fields copied as a starting
             point, independently editable/re-lockable
        3. unit_change_requests.new_transaction_id populated
           — old and new transactions now navigable both ways:
           forward via unit_change_requests, backward via
           new_transaction.source_transaction_id
        Before step 2, the API validates: old txn still ACTIVE, request
        still PENDING, new unit exists and has no ACTIVE transaction of
        its own — rejecting with a clear error otherwise, not just
        relying on the database's partial unique index to fail loudly.
    end note

    note left of Requested
        Old unit's status recomputes once its transaction
        is SUPERSEDED, same trigger as Cancellation —
        SUPERSEDED and CANCELLED are both excluded from
        the "one ACTIVE transaction per unit" index.
    end note
